WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:00.745
Part two.

00:00:00.745 --> 00:00:05.375
Building your résumé, and formatting it for wherever you are applying.

00:00:05.675 --> 00:00:07.978
Here is the part nobody tells you.

00:00:07.978 --> 00:00:12.315
Before a person ever reads your résumé, software reads it first,

00:00:12.315 --> 00:00:18.006
and it is trying to pull out your name, your dates, your job titles and your skills.

00:00:18.006 --> 00:00:20.242
The handsome two column template,

00:00:20.242 --> 00:00:25.324
with the icons and the little skill bars, is exactly where that goes wrong.

00:00:25.324 --> 00:00:27.560
So the app rates every one of its

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.880
formats on how cleanly it reads to that software.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.929
The five star ones are built to be understood

00:00:33.929 --> 00:00:37.588
by the machine first, and admired by the human second.

00:00:37.888 --> 00:00:40.754
Open Resume Builder, and tell it your story.

00:00:41.054 --> 00:00:46.069
Paste in anything. An old résumé, a LinkedIn bio, rough notes.

00:00:46.369 --> 00:00:49.719
What comes back is structured, not just tidied up.

00:00:50.019 --> 00:00:54.200
And a résumé is not the same document everywhere in the world.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:59.056
A German CV is expected to carry a photo. A US résumé must not have one.

00:00:59.056 --> 00:01:04.586
India, the United Kingdom, Singapore, the European Union — each has its own shape,

00:01:04.586 --> 00:01:11.734
and getting it wrong is the sort of thing that quietly costs you an interview, and you never find out why.

00:01:11.734 --> 00:01:16.387
Choose your region here, and the whole document is rebuilt to fit it.

00:01:16.687 --> 00:01:21.375
Swipe the formats for your region. Each one carries that star rating.

00:01:21.675 --> 00:01:24.716
Then download it as a PDF or a Word file.

00:01:25.016 --> 00:01:28.807
Next, finding a job — and handing the research over.

